Q: Check if StreamSubscription is canceled A: <p>In addition to the solutions already proposed, there is another way how this can be done.</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>import 'dart:async';

import 'package:multitasking/multitasking.dart';

void main(List&lt;String&gt; args) async {
  var state1 = 'running';
  var state2 = 'running';
  var state3 = 'running';
  final stream1 = Stream.periodic(Duration(seconds: 1), (count) {
    return count;
  }).withCancellationHandler(() {
    state1 = 'canceled';
  });

  final sub1 = stream1.listen(print);

  final stream2 = Stream.periodic(Duration(seconds: 1), (count) {
    return count;
  }).withCancellationHandler(() {
    state2 = 'canceled';
  });

  unawaited(() async {
    await for (final event in stream2) {
      print(event);
      if (event &gt; 2) {
        break;
      }
    }
  }());

  final stream3 = Stream.periodic(Duration(seconds: 1), (count) {
    return count;
  });

  final sub3 = stream3.listenWithCancellationHandler(print, onCancel: () {
    state3 = 'canceled';
  });

  await Future&lt;void&gt;.delayed(Duration(seconds: 4));
  await sub1.cancel();
  await sub3.cancel();
  print(state1);
  print(state2);
  print(state3);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Results:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>0
0
0
1
1
1
2
2
2
3
3
canceled
canceled
canceled
</code></pre>
<p>P.S.</p>
<p>This only works if the subscription was canceled forcibly (by the consumer).<br />
If the stream is closed without any cancellation calls (by the producer), this won't work.</p>
